# React Maintenance

- Centralize repeated third-party setup in `src/components/common/` instead of
  re-registering modules in feature components.
- Avoid one-line feature re-export files unless they document a real feature
  boundary or add behavior.
- Prefer canonical constants for resume/profile facts; do not duplicate titles,
  locations, schools, or other stable facts in components.
- Give custom clickable elements and icon-only controls accessible names with
  `aria-label` or visible text.
- Keep render-time objects and lookups stable when they are reused in maps or
  memoized children. For example, hoist sentinel objects and use `Set` for
  repeated membership checks.
- When cleaning redundancy, favor small shared helpers that match existing
  patterns over broad rewrites.
- When removing a feature, remove its route/menu entry, colocated tests,
  feature constants/state, and coverage metadata in the same cleanup.
- Treat generated deployment output as outside normal maintenance; do not touch
  `docs/` while cleaning source code.
- Use named exports only for cross-file APIs. Convert local-only helpers,
  constants, and prop types to non-exported declarations instead of leaving a
  broad public surface.
- Run the knip check after removals and export cleanup so stale files,
  unused exports, and dependency drift are caught before finishing.
- Don't add `useMemo`/`useCallback`/`React.memo` by default. Each one has
  comparison overhead, so only keep it when it earns that cost:
  - `React.memo` on a zero-prop component can still pay off when the parent
    re-renders often and the child's subtree is costly — shallow compare sees
    no prop changes and skips the re-render. Don't add it by default, but
    don't strip it without weighing parent render frequency and subtree cost.
  - `React.memo` is useless on a component whose props are recreated fresh
    every render by the parent (inline object, array, or function literals;
    curried handlers like `onClick={handleClick(id)}`) — the identity check
    always fails, so the memo never bails out. Either give the parent a
    `useCallback`/stable reference, or drop the `memo`.
  - `useCallback`/`useMemo` can pay off for dependency or subscription
    identity stability alone — not only when recomputation is expensive.
    Inline functions or objects passed as `useEffect`/`useLayoutEffect`
    dependencies get a new identity every render and can trigger repeated
    cleanup and setup; when stable identity is not required, move the value
    into the effect body or depend on primitive values instead. For
    `memo`-wrapped children, stable references still prevent unnecessary
    re-renders; `useMemo` can also cache expensive calculations within the
    same component when dependencies are unchanged. Avoid these hooks when
    the function/value is only used inline in the same render and its only
    consumer is not memoized.
  - Don't wrap an already-stable reference (a module-level constant, an
    imported function) in `useMemo`/`useCallback` — it adds a dependency
    check for a value that never changes.
  - Don't memoize trivially cheap computations (simple arithmetic, string
    concatenation, small object literals) — the memo's own comparison
    costs more than just recomputing.
  - When memoizing a callback for a memoized child, include every reactive
    dependency in the dependency array — including unstable values. Do not
    omit changing dependencies merely to preserve callback identity;
    dependency stability is an optimization, not a correctness requirement.
    A `useCallback` that returns a curried closure
    (`useCallback((id) => () => {...}, [...])`) still produces a new inner
    function per render — the outer memoization doesn't help the child that
    receives the curried result.
